# SvelteKit `$app/state`

Cursor: `@sveltejs/kit` 2.12 introduced `$app/state` as a runes-aware replacement for `$app/stores`. The Svelte 4 store form (`$page`) still works for backward compatibility but is no longer the documented shape. Target: `@sveltejs/kit ^2.60.1`.

The companion `sveltekit-anti-patterns` rule rejects `$app/stores` in new code (entry #22). This rule explains the positive form.

## `page` from `$app/state`

`page` is a reactive object. Read fields directly; Svelte 5 tracks them.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { page } from '$app/state'
</script>

<p>Path: {page.url.pathname}</p>
<p>Search: {page.url.searchParams.get('q')}</p>
<p>Slug: {page.params.slug}</p>
<p>Route id: {page.route.id}</p>
<p>Status: {page.status}</p>
{#if page.error}<pre>{page.error.message}</pre>{/if}
```

`page.data` is the merged `load` data (same as the `data` prop, but accessible from layouts and nested components).

`page.form` is the most recent form action result.

`page.state` is the shallow-routing state (set via `pushState` / `replaceState`).

## `navigating` from `$app/state`

`navigating` is a reactive object whose fields are `null` when no navigation is in progress (and during server rendering). The object itself is always truthy, so `{#if navigating}` is NOT a valid "is navigating" check. Guard on `navigating.type` instead.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { navigating } from '$app/state'
</script>

{#if navigating.type}
  <progress />
  <p>From {navigating.from?.url.pathname} to {navigating.to?.url.pathname}</p>
{/if}
```

Fields:

- `navigating.from` - the URL we are navigating from
- `navigating.to` - the URL we are navigating to
- `navigating.type` - `'enter' | 'leave' | 'link' | 'goto' | 'popstate' | 'form' | null`
- `navigating.willUnload` - true if leaving the app
- `navigating.delta` - browser history delta for `popstate`
- `navigating.complete` - a promise that resolves when the navigation finishes

## `updated` from `$app/state`

Tracks whether a new app version has been deployed (uses the version polling configured in `kit.version`).

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { updated } from '$app/state'
</script>

{#if updated.current}
  <button onclick={() => location.reload()}>New version available - reload</button>
{/if}
```

## Use anywhere

`$app/state` reads work in `<script>`, in template, in `.svelte.ts` modules, and in callbacks. There is no need to subscribe / unsubscribe.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { page } from '$app/state'
  import { goto } from '$app/navigation'

  function searchAgain() {
    const q = page.url.searchParams.get('q')
    if (q) goto(`/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}&again=1`)
  }
</script>
```

## Migration cheat sheet

| `$app/stores` (Svelte 4 form)        | `$app/state` (Svelte 5 form)         |
| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `$page.url.pathname`                  | `page.url.pathname`                   |
| `$page.params.slug`                   | `page.params.slug`                    |
| `$page.data`                          | `page.data`                           |
| `$page.error`                         | `page.error`                          |
| `$page.form`                          | `page.form`                           |
| `$navigating?.to.url.pathname`        | `navigating.to?.url.pathname`         |
| `$updated`                            | `updated.current`                     |

## Why these matter for LLM output

Pre-2025 trained models will produce `import { page } from '$app/stores'` and `$page.url.pathname`. The `$app/stores` form still works but is deprecated for new code as of 2.12 and prints a console warning in dev (anti-pattern #22).
